---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import SectionNav from '../../components/SectionNav.astro';
---
<Base title={"Why Banks Sell Hotel Loans"} description={"A hotel workout asks more of a lender than most banks are staffed for. How a loan sale compares with extending, foreclosing, and a discounted payoff."}>
  <Hero eyebrow={"For banks"} title={"Why banks sell hotel loans."} lead={"A hotel workout asks more of a lender than most banks are staffed for. A loan sale ends it on a date the bank chooses."} />
  <SectionNav section="banks" />
  <main id="main">
<section class="section"><div class="wrap intro">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">The burden</p><h2>A hotel is a business, and a workout has to deal with all of it.</h2>
        <div class="body"><p>Enforcing a hotel loan means dealing with a franchisor, a management company, a liquor authority, and a payroll, usually through a receiver, for as long as foreclosure takes in that state.</p>
        <p>The collateral is an operating business. Its value rests on the franchise, the staff, the bookings, and the licences, and it can fall quickly while a workout runs.</p>
        <p>For the bank, a classified hotel loan ties up the special assets group, reserves, and management attention for as long as the workout runs.</p></div></div>
      <dl class="pillars">
      <div><dt>The workout is consuming staff time</dt><dd>One hotel loan can occupy a credit officer for a year or more.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>The hotel needs renovations nobody will fund</dt><dd>The brand's improvement plan is overdue, and the bank does not want to advance the cost.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>The bank does not want to own a hotel</dt><dd>Taking title means operating a business the bank has no licence, staff, or wish to run.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>The borrower is a customer</dt><dd>A sale lets someone else do the enforcing.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    </div></section>

    <section class="section alt"><div class="wrap">
      <div class="head"><p class="eyebrow">The choices</p><h2>Five ways to resolve a classified hotel loan.</h2>
        <p>Each has a cost that does not appear in the loan balance. The right answer depends on how much has been reserved against the loan, how long enforcement takes in that state, and where the hotel is heading while the process runs.</p></div>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">What it does</th><th scope="col">What it costs the bank</th><th scope="col">When it fits</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Modify, extend, or forbear</th><td>Keeps the customer and defers the loss.</td><td>The loan stays classified and keeps taking up capital and staff attention. If the hotel does not recover, the same decision returns later, with an older property and a less patient brand.</td><td>The hotel is viable and the owner is capable and cooperative.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Discounted payoff</th><td>Ends the loan at an agreed figure, in exchange for payment now.</td><td>A discount to the balance, and it needs someone with the money to close it.</td><td>The owner can raise new capital or refinance part of the balance.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Foreclose and hold as OREO</th><td>Can produce the highest recovery on paper.</td><td>It takes the longest, especially where foreclosure runs through the courts, and it makes the bank the owner of an operating business, an <a href="/glossary/#oreo">OREO</a> hotel it must staff, license, insure, and eventually sell.</td><td>No agreement can be reached.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Receivership</th><td>A court-appointed receiver operates the hotel while the bank decides between selling the loan and foreclosure.</td><td>Receiver costs and any protective advances, and the case can run for a long time before a sale closes.</td><td>The hotel has to keep operating while the enforcement process runs.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Sell the loan</th><td>Fixes the recovery on a known date.</td><td>The price reflects the time and risk the buyer takes on, so it is lower than a successful workout might yield.</td><td>Another party is better placed to own the loan. In exchange the bank is finished: no legal budget, no protective advances, no hotel to run.</td></tr>
        </tbody></table></div>
    </div></section>

    <section class="section"><div class="wrap">
      <div class="head"><p class="eyebrow">What a sale changes</p><h2>For the bank, the problem ends on a date it chooses.</h2></div>
      <div class="cells">
        <div><h3>A date the bank chooses</h3><p>A loan sale ends the bank's involvement on a date it chooses, instead of when a foreclosure or a receivership concludes.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Cash at closing</h3><p>The bank receives cash at closing and transfers the loan, the file, and the workout.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Off the problem list</h3><p>The loan, the file, and the workout leave the bank. No legal budget, no protective advances, and no business to staff, license, and insure.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">How a sale is treated in the bank's accounts and with its regulator is a matter for the bank and its advisors.</p>
    </div></section>

    <section class="section alt"><div class="wrap">
      <div class="head"><p class="eyebrow">Where a sale fits</p><h2>The course of <em>a defaulted hotel loan.</em></h2>
        <p>A lender can sell at any stage. The earlier the sale, the less time and cost it carries.</p></div>
      <div class="tl">
        <div class="step"><h3>Default</h3><p>A payment is missed or the loan matures unpaid.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><h3>Forbearance</h3><p>Lender and owner try to agree on new terms.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><h3>Receivership</h3><p>A court appoints a receiver to operate the hotel.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><h3>Foreclosure</h3><p>The lender enforces its lien on the property.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><h3>Lender-owned</h3><p>The lender holds title and runs the hotel.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><h3>Sale</h3><p>The hotel passes to a new owner.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="ranges">
        <div class="r1"><strong>We buy the loan</strong><span>At any point before the lender takes title</span></div>
        <div class="r2"><strong>We buy the hotel</strong><span>Through a receiver's sale, at foreclosure, or from the lender</span></div>
      </div>
    </div></section>

    <section class="section alt"><div class="wrap"><div class="head"><p class="eyebrow">Related reading</p></div><ul class="toc"><li><a href="/library/choices/">Extend, sell, or foreclose <span>Library</span></a></li><li><a href="/blog/foreclosure-timelines-and-hotel-value/">Foreclosure timelines and why they matter more for hotels <span>Blog</span></a></li><li><a href="/blog/discounted-payoffs-on-hotel-loans/">Discounted payoffs on hotel loans: what a lender should weigh <span>Blog</span></a></li><li><a href="/glossary/#non-performing-loan">Non-performing loan (NPL) <span>Glossary</span></a></li></ul></div></section>
  </main>
  <Cta title={"Discuss a hotel loan in confidence."} text={"A conversation and an indicative price carry no obligation to sell."} label={"Start a conversation"} />
</Base>
